<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siraj ud-Daulah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siraj ud-Daulah was the last independent Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, succeeding his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a> in 1756. In June that year he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, and the deaths of British prisoners in the 'Black Hole' were used to justify reprisals. Betrayed by his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> at Plassey in 1757 and was captured and killed soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah.jpg" alt="Siraj ud-Daulah" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siraj_ud-Daulah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siraj ud-Daulah was born in 1733 to Mirza Muhammad Hashim and Amina Begum, the youngest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a>. He was raised at the Nawab's palace with the education suited to a future Nawab and accompanied Alivardi on campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> in 1746. In 1750 he revolted against his grandfather and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, but he soon surrendered and was forgiven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Siraj succeeded his maternal grandfather in April 1756, at the age of 23. His nomination aroused the jealousy of his aunt Ghaseti Begum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-seth">Jagat Seth</a> and his cousin Shaukat Jang, the governor of Purnia, who was killed in a clash with him. Siraj seized Ghaseti Begum's wealth and replaced Mir Jafar as paymaster of the army with Mir Madan. He resented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s growing presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and charged it with strengthening Fort William without approval, abusing trade privileges and sheltering some of his officers.</p><p>When the Company went on strengthening Fort William despite his order to stop, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in June 1756, briefly renaming it Alinagar. The British captives were put in a prison cell, and in the confusion of command they were left there overnight and many died. In the parliamentary inquiry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>, Sir William Meredith cleared Siraj of any blame for the Black Hole.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A conspiracy formed at his court, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, Rai Durlabh, Yar Lutuf Khan, Omichund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-seth">Jagat Seth</a>, and the Company signed a treaty with Mir Jafar on 4 June 1757 to put him on the throne. At Plassey on 23 June Mir Jafar advised Siraj to halt the fighting for the day, and when the soldiers turned back to camp Clive attacked and the army fell into disorder. Siraj fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> and then tried to escape towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> by boat, but was captured. Mir Jafar's son Mir Miran ordered his execution, carried out on 2 July 1757. His tomb is at Khushbagh, Murshidabad.</p><p>He has a positive reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> for opposing the start of British rule, and as the independence movement grew he was counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> among those who resisted imperial aggression. The historian Sushil Chaudhary argued that his villainous image is a misrepresentation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siraj-ud-Daulah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Helped by the defection of Mir Jafar, Clive's East India Company troops defeated <b>Siraj ud-Daulah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-23">23 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Siraj ud-Daulah</b> attacked Calcutta in 1756.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.18,88.27&amp;year=1748" aria-label="Murshidabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6899 1273 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Siraj ud-Daulah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7349 1543h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7349 1543h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.18,88.27&amp;year=1748">What was here in 1748? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
